<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Óengus I of the Picts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 761</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Óengus son of Fergus, Óengus mac Fergusa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Óengus son of Fergus was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> from 729 to 761 and the most powerful ruler in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> of his time. Emerging from civil wars in Pictland, he defeated his rivals, campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, whose stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a> he took in 736, and fought Strathclyde. The Pictish over-kingship based in Fortriu reached its height under him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts.jpg" alt="Óengus I of the Picts" width="310" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Son of Groucho of Scotland on Flikr. · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%93engus_detail_sarcophagus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Óengus appears to have been a native of the Mearns, Pictish Circin, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a> and the Mearns. An early medieval Irish genealogy made him a descendant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a> of Mag Gergind and kin to the Eóganachta of Munster, but the link was likely invented as propaganda for an alliance around 735 between Óengus and Cathal, king of Munster. He was middle-aged by the time he entered the record. His close kin included two sons, Bridei (died 736) and Talorgan (died 782), and two brothers, Talorgan (died 750) and Bridei (died 763).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nechtan-mac-der-ilei">Nechtan son of Der-Ilei</a> abdicated in 724 and was imprisoned by his successor Drest, four kings contended for power in 728 and 729: Drest, Nechtan, Alpín and Óengus, a partisan of Nechtan and perhaps his heir. Óengus defeated Alpín twice, first at the hill of Moncrieffe near Perth, and his supporters won at Monith Carno. On 12 August 729 he defeated and killed Drest at Druimm Derg Blathuug. Nechtan was restored, probably until his death in 732.</p><p>In the 730s Óengus turned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, whose Irish protectors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-conaill">Cenél Conaill</a>, were weakened. Talorgan mac Congussa was drowned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> in 734, and in 736 Dúngal mac Selbaig and his brother Feradach were captured and the fortress of Creic burnt with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a>. A final campaign in 741, the smiting, left Dál Riata absent from the record for a generation. War with Northumbria followed in 740, and with the Britons of Alt Clut in 744 and 750, when his brother Talorgan was killed and the annals recorded the ebbing of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. In 756 he led an army with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadberht-of-northumbria">Eadberht of Northumbria</a> to Dumbarton, where the Britons accepted terms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Óengus died in 761, aged probably more than seventy. The continuator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> wrote that from the beginning of his reign to the end he &quot;perpetrated bloody crimes&quot;. His brother Bridei succeeded him, and his son Talorgan reigned from around 780 until 782. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>, then Cennrígmonaid, was founded before 747, probably by Óengus or by Nechtan, and the St Andrews Sarcophagus is generally presumed to have been made at his command. Kings of his family ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> until a defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> in 839. The historian Murray Pittock has argued that his rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> north of the Forth foreshadowed a future united Scottish kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Óengus I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 730s and 740s the Pictish king <b>Óengus son of Fergus</b> attacked Dál Riata, capturing Dunadd in 736.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings fought one another in recurring civil wars, and <b>Óengus son of Fergus</b> rose to power through a series of battles in 728–729 before attacking Dál Riata, whose stronghold of Dunadd he took in 736.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the reign of Bridei mac Beli, who defeated the Anglians at the Battle of Dun Nechtain in 685. <b>Óengus mac Fergusa</b> (reigned 729–761) held Dál Riata subject, and Caustantín mac Fergusa (793–820) placed his son Domnall on its throne, though Pictish attempts to dominate the Britons of Alt…</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Selbach's defeat at sea in 719 and Pictish conquest under <b>Oengus son of Fergus</b> in the 730s broke Cenel Loairn's power.</blockquote><a href="/state/cenel-loairn">Cenel Loairn</a></li><li><blockquote>Atholl, Athfotla, appears in Irish annals from the 8th century, when a king of Atholl was drowned by <b>Oengus son of Fergus</b>, king of the Picts, in 739.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-atholl">Kingdom of Atholl</a></li><li><blockquote>Bridei son of Beli destroyed the Northumbrian army at Dun Nechtain in 685, ending English dominance in the north. <b>Óengus son of Fergus</b>, from 729 to 761, defeated rivals and even campaigned against Dál Riata and Strathclyde.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fortriu">Kingdom of Fortriu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.34,-2.8&amp;year=751" aria-label="St Andrews on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Óengus I of the Picts"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4867 5310h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4867 5310h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=56.34,-2.8&amp;year=751">What was here in 751 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
